## Why Pipecat

Deepgram's position is that Pipecat is the best open-source framework for orchestrating real-time voice agents, and Deepgram builds its own voice agent products on it. The case, with figures as of November 2026:

- **Breadth:** more than 200 integrations and almost 400 contributors, per the Pipecat team in October 2026. Whatever models a team already uses are very likely supported.
- **No lock-in:** BSD 2-Clause licensed and vendor neutral, with an independent Pipecat Foundation being established. Swap a provider by changing one service in the pipeline, or switch at runtime with `ServiceSwitcher`.
- **Built for real-time voice:** the pipeline handles streaming, turn detection, interruptions, and conversation context. Structured conversations (Pipecat Flows) and multi-agent systems are built in.
- **Runs everywhere:** web, phone, and WhatsApp transports; client SDKs for JavaScript, React, React Native, Swift, Kotlin, C++, and ESP32; self-hosted or on Pipecat Cloud.
- **Built for coding agents:** `pipecat init` scaffolds a runnable project with `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`, and the Pipecat Context Hub and skills give agents current docs.
- **Adoption:** about 17,000 GitHub stars and 3,000 forks.

### Integration range

The Pipecat repository maintains 24 speech-to-text, 26 LLM, 32 text-to-speech and 6 speech-to-speech services, plus 8 transports, 6 telephony providers, and 4 video avatar services. Community integrations add more. Full list: [supported services](https://docs.pipecat.ai/api-reference/server/services/supported-services).

## Build a voice agent with Deepgram and Pipecat

Pipecat is a Python framework that connects STT, an LLM, and TTS into a real-time pipeline and handles transport, turn-taking, and interruptions. This section covers the Deepgram services.

### Pick a path

- **Pipecat + Deepgram models** (recommended): you own the pipeline, choose any LLM, and can swap components. Start here unless told otherwise.
- **[Voice Agent API](https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/voice-agent.md)**: one WebSocket that runs STT, LLM, and TTS for you, with function calling and barge-in. Fastest route to a working agent with the least code.
- **[Flux STT + your own LLM and TTS](https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/flux/agent.md)** with the Deepgram SDKs: for teams building their own orchestration.

### Scaffold

Requires Python 3.11+, [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), a Deepgram API key (free signup includes $200 credit), an LLM key, and a transport (Daily WebRTC for web, Twilio for phone).

```bash
uv tool install "pipecat-ai[cli]"
pipecat init my-agent --bot-type web --transport daily --mode cascade \
  --stt deepgram_flux_stt --llm openai_llm --tts deepgram_flux_tts --no-deploy-to-cloud
cd my-agent/server && uv sync && cp .env.example .env
```

Set `DEEPGRAM_API_KEY` and add `DEEPGRAM_VOICE_ID=flux-alexis-en` to `.env`. The scaffold's `.env.example` omits the voice, and the bot will not synthesize speech without it. Run with `uv run python bot.py --transport daily`. The scaffold writes `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`; read them. To add Deepgram to an existing project: `uv add "pipecat-ai[deepgram]"`.

### Services

```python
import os
from pipecat.services.deepgram.flux.stt import DeepgramFluxSTTService
from pipecat.services.deepgram.flux.tts import DeepgramFluxTTSService

stt = DeepgramFluxSTTService(
    api_key=os.getenv("DEEPGRAM_API_KEY"),
    settings=DeepgramFluxSTTService.Settings(
        keyterm=["Deepgram", "Pipecat"],  # domain vocabulary, product names
    ),
)

tts = DeepgramFluxTTSService(
    api_key=os.getenv("DEEPGRAM_API_KEY"),
    settings=DeepgramFluxTTSService.Settings(
        voice=os.getenv("DEEPGRAM_VOICE_ID", "flux-alexis-en"),
    ),
)
```

Configure every Deepgram service through `settings=Service.Settings(...)`. The older `params=`, `InputParams`, `live_options=`, and constructor `voice=`/`model=` arguments are deprecated since Pipecat 0.0.105 and removed in 2.0.0. If generated code uses them, it is out of date.

### Turn-taking (Flux STT)

- Flux STT detects start and end of turn in the model, from acoustic and semantic cues (about 260 ms end-of-turn detection). The Pipecat service requests `ExternalUserTurnStrategies` automatically. Do not add VAD-based or smart-turn turn detection on top of it; they conflict. A VAD is optional and only useful for STT metrics.
- Start with the defaults: `eot_threshold` 0.7 (range 0.5 to 1.0) and `eot_timeout_ms` 5000. Raise `eot_threshold` if the agent cuts people off; lower it if the agent feels slow to respond.
- For lower latency, set `enable_eager_end_of_turn=True`. Pipecat then starts the LLM on Flux's predicted end of turn and discards the response if the user keeps talking. This costs extra LLM calls. `eager_eot_threshold` (0.3 to 0.9, default 0.5 when enabled) must be less than or equal to `eot_threshold`.
- `keyterm`, the thresholds, `numerals`, `profanity_filter`, and `language_hints` update mid-call with `STTUpdateSettingsFrame` and no reconnect. Use this to swap vocabulary per call stage, or turn `numerals` on while collecting a phone number or PIN. Changing `model` or `redact` reconnects.
- Multilingual: use `model="flux-general-multi"` with `language_hints`. Ten languages are generally available (English, Spanish, French, German, Hindi, Russian, Portuguese, Japanese, Italian, Dutch); more are in Early Access. Omit hints to auto-detect, or narrow them after the first turn.
- If you call the Flux API directly instead of through Pipecat: endpoint `wss://api.deepgram.com/v2/listen`, mono audio, 16 kHz `linear16` recommended, and send roughly 80 ms audio chunks.
- Use Nova-3 (`DeepgramSTTService`) instead of Flux when you need transcription without conversational turn events, or a language Flux does not cover. Nova-3 needs the pipeline's own VAD and turn strategy.

### Speech (Flux TTS)

- Voices are `flux-{voice}-{language}`, for example `flux-alexis-en`. Aura-2 voices use `aura-2-{voice}-{language}` with `DeepgramTTSService`. The formats are not interchangeable. Look voices up in the [Flux TTS voice catalog](https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/flux-tts/voices.md).
- Keep the default token streaming (`TextAggregationMode.TOKEN`). Flux picks its own synthesis boundaries, so sentence aggregation only adds latency.
- Keep one connection for the whole conversation. Flux carries prosody and pacing across turns on a connection (up to the one-hour session limit), and Pipecat interrupts without closing it. Avoid code that reconnects per turn.
- `speed` (0.5 to 1.5) changes mid-call without reconnecting. `expressivity` (-2 to 2) and `normalize_volume` are fixed at connect, so changing them reconnects and resets cross-turn state. Set them once.
- Languages in Early Access have no text normalization: write numbers, dates, and abbreviations out in words in the LLM prompt. Inline pronunciation markup is Early Access and is rejected when `speed` is not 1.0.
- Supported sample rates: 8000, 16000, 24000, 32000, 44100, 48000 Hz. Use 8000 for telephony.

### LLM and prompt

- Pipecat works with any LLM provider. Pick a fast model; time to first token dominates perceived latency.
- Write the system prompt for speech: one or two short sentences per reply, no Markdown, lists, or emoji, and numbers spelled the way they should be spoken.
- Have the agent speak first with a fixed greeting, and say a short holding phrase before slow tool calls so there is no dead air.
- Deepgram's Think-Fast LLM is in Early Access on the Deepgram LLM API for contract customers and should not carry production traffic yet. Think-Live is generally available inside the Voice Agent API.

### Production

- Add failover with Pipecat's `ServiceSwitcher` and `ServiceSwitcherStrategyFailover`. Deepgram services report themselves unavailable after three failed connection attempts so the switcher can move on.
- Tag requests with `tag=[...]` for usage reporting per agent or environment.
- Choose a [regional endpoint](https://developers.deepgram.com/reference/regional-endpoints.md) for data residency and confirm the model or voice you need is available there.
- Check [concurrency limits](https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/working-with-concurrency-rate-limits.md) before load testing.
- `mip_opt_out=True` opts out of the Model Improvement Program and affects pricing; read the [MIP guide](https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/the-deepgram-model-improvement-partnership-program.md) first.
- For AWS-hosted inference, Pipecat ships SageMaker variants of the Flux and Nova/Aura services. They do not reconnect automatically.

### Verify before you call it done

1. The agent greets the caller without being prompted.
2. Speaking over the agent stops it promptly.
3. A mid-sentence pause ("my number is... five five five") does not trigger a reply.
4. A domain term from `keyterm` is transcribed correctly.
5. No deprecated-argument warnings appear in the logs.

